// Lesson 8.1a — Boss Design: Spec It First
// Write the boss spec BEFORE building. Phases, tells, health economy.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>8.1a — Boss Design: Spec It First</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    "Sorta their first on their own experience" — this is the
    capstone. The mistake every first-time boss builder makes: start
    with the boss's SIZE. Today you start with its SPEC — a one-page
    design document, in words, that a stranger could build from.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A boss is a conversation: it asks a question with an attack,
        the player answers with a move, and the boss listens (takes
    </p>
    <p><strong>The spec sheet (write all six, today, on paper):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Identity (1 sentence):</strong> "The Cave Warden:
            slow, huge, guards the exit; telegraphs everything."</li>
        <li><strong>Health economy:</strong> boss HP vs. how many hits
            your player can deal (<a href="8.3a.html">8.3a</a>) vs.
            player HP 3. Aim: a fight of 60–90 seconds. Do the
            arithmetic NOW: hits needed × seconds between openings =
            fight length</li>
        <li><strong>Phases (2 or 3):</strong> what changes at each
            threshold — new attack? faster? arena change? Each phase
            must be <em>readable</em> by the player within one
            exchange</li>
        <li><strong>Attacks (2–3 per phase max), each with:</strong>
            telegraph (what tells you it is coming — wind-up frames,
            a flash), timing (how long to react), counterplay (the
            move that beats it), and punishment (what a mistake
            costs)</li>
        <li><strong>Tells &amp; fairness:</strong> same rule as
            <a href="7.5a.html">7.5a</a> signposting — the boss is a
            level that fights back, so it must be readable</li>
        <li><strong>Arena:</strong> reuse your Unit 6 "room" shape:
            two exits becomes one exit (the door only opens when the
            boss dies); hazards the BOSS ignores but the player
            must respect</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The AP exam's Create PT asks you to justify design
            decisions in writing. Boss specs ARE that skill. Why does
            writing the spec BEFORE the code make the coding shorter
            — and what kind of bug does a spec prevent (hint: the
            kind where the boss is simultaneously too strong to
            lose to and too boring to win against)?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Design Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Stress-test the spec with the enemy state machine.</strong>
    Your boss is <a href="4.5a.html">4.5a</a>'s machine with more
    states. Map it — states as boxes, thresholds on the arrows:</p>
    <pre><code>IDLE (2s) → ATTACK 1 (telegraph 0.6s, strike, recover 1s)
    → IDLE → ATTACK 2 ...
HP &lt;= 50%  → PHASE 2 (attack cooldown 2s → 1.2s, adds ATTACK 3)
HP &lt;= 20%  → PHASE 3 (speed 2 → 3, arena hazard activates)
HP &lt;= 0    → DEAD (door opens)</code></pre>
    <p>
    Count your boxes: a boss with 6 states is a big build; with 12
    it will not exist by January. Cut states until it is buildable,
    not until it is impressive. You can always add a phase after it
    WORKS — and never before.</p>

    <p><strong>The one question that catches bad specs:</strong> for
    each attack, what does the player DO? If the answer is "dodge
    randomly" or "take the hit," the attack is decoration. Every
    attack needs its counterplay sentence: "jump over the shockwave;
    the jump is possible because the wave is 1 tile tall and your
    jump is 2."</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Spec review</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Swap specs with a partner. They play the boss IN
                THEIR HEAD against your attack list — ask them to
                say the counterplay for each. Any attack they
                cannot answer is under-designed.</li>
            <li>Check the arithmetic: if the player can deal damage
                only 1 second out of every 5, and the boss needs 20
                hits... what is the FIGHT LENGTH? Over 90 seconds
                means cut HP or add openings.</li>
            <li>Phase 3 adds speed AND a hazard AND a new attack.
                How many new things did you introduce at once —
                what does <a href="7.5a.html">7.5a</a> say about
                that number?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Commit the design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Type the final spec as <code>BOSS_SPEC.md</code> in
                your repo root, next to the code it will govern.
                Design docs live WITH the project.</li>
            <li>Sketch the arena on paper (reuse the Unit 6 room);
                mark where the player enters, where the boss
                idles, and the door that stays shut.</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"8.1a: boss spec + arena sketch"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write a six-part boss spec: identity, economy, phases, attacks, tells, arena</li>
            <li>Do the health arithmetic and defend the fight length</li>
            <li>Map the boss as a state machine with thresholds on the arrows</li>
            <li>Answer "what does the player DO?" for every single attack</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

